# Who must review what.
#
# This is not a statement about who is trusted. It is a list of the places where
# a mistake is expensive or hard to undo, and it exists so that a well-meaning
# change to one of them cannot merge without the person who knows why it is
# shaped that way.
#
# CODEOWNERS only takes effect once branch protection requires review from code
# owners — see SECURITY.md §"Repository settings".

# Default: nothing is owned, so ordinary app and UI work needs no special review.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MIGRATIONS — the sharpest edge in the repo.
#
# This product is DEPLOYED, with real camps and real people in it. Migrations are
# append-only and the deploy applies them automatically. A migration merged here
# runs against production on the next deploy; there is no staging step that would
# catch it first, and an already-applied migration cannot be edited — only
# corrected by another one.
/packages/db/migrations/  @BeyersNel
/packages/db/src/schema.ts @BeyersNel

# AUTH — sessions, passwords, two-factor, passkeys, the cookie handling.
# Self-hosted Better Auth pinned to an exact version on purpose: it has a track
# record of high-severity advisories and we own the patch watch.
/packages/auth/           @BeyersNel

# CORE — the domain rules every app reads. Permissions, privacy classes, the
# registration state machine, the submit gate. A change here changes behaviour in
# three apps at once, usually without touching any of them.
/packages/core/           @BeyersNel

# CI, licence, and the rules themselves.
/.github/                 @BeyersNel
/LICENSE                  @BeyersNel
/AGENTS.md                @BeyersNel
/CONTRIBUTING.md          @BeyersNel
/SECURITY.md              @BeyersNel
/turbo.json               @BeyersNel

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PUBLISHED SDK AND THE EXTERNAL API — irrevocable in a way nothing else is.
#
# An Apache-2.0 grant on a published version cannot be withdrawn; a scope string
# that shipped is a promise to code in repositories we do not control; and a /v1
# handler is the one place data leaves the platform to a party that holds no
# membership. A migration can at least be corrected by another migration.
#
# None of these paths exist yet (docs/sdk/), and the entries are inert until
# branch protection requires code-owner review. Both are the point: the rule
# should be in place before the first file lands, not written afterwards.
/packages/scopes/              @BeyersNel
/packages/sdk/                 @BeyersNel
/packages/sdk-react/           @BeyersNel
/apps/web/app/api/v1/          @BeyersNel
/apps/web/middleware.ts        @BeyersNel
/.changeset/                   @BeyersNel
/docs/sdk/                     @BeyersNel
